{#
   The head of a listing: the front page, a section, a label, the search results.

   One component because those four screens are the same thing — a title over a
   list of articles — and until it existed they each said so differently. A
   section had a bordered block and a description; a label had a small grey
   eyebrow above its name; the search page had a bare heading; the front page had
   nothing at all. Four treatments for one idea, with no decision behind any of
   the differences.

   Everything but `title` is optional, so a screen that has no description or no
   sub-links renders without a gap where they would be.

     eyebrow  — what kind of listing this is, when that is not obvious
     title    — required
     lead     — a sentence about it
     links    — a list of { label, path }, for a section's subsections
#}
<header class="border-b border-rule pb-6">
    {% if eyebrow|default('') %}
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">{{ eyebrow }}</p>
    {% endif %}

    <h1 class="mt-1 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">{{ title }}</h1>

    {% if lead|default('') %}
        <p class="mt-2 max-w-prose text-muted">{{ lead }}</p>
    {% endif %}

    {% if links|default([]) is not empty %}
        <ul class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1 text-sm">
            {% for link in links %}
                <li>
                    <a href="{{ link.path }}"
                       class="text-accent no-underline hover:underline">{{ link.label }}</a>
                </li>
            {% endfor %}
        </ul>
    {% endif %}
</header>
